This bibliometric analysis focuses on 228 COVID-19–related publications indexed in PubMed with relevance to **Kenya**, offering insights into national research trends, contributing authors, and thematic priorities. The peak in publication activity occurred between 2020 and 2022, reflecting the urgency of Kenya’s research response during the height of the pandemic. Prominent journals such as *BMC Infectious Diseases*, *PLOS ONE*, and *BMJ Global Health* frequently published Kenyan studies, particularly those related to public health, vaccine uptake, and community interventions. Authorship analysis revealed a concentration of publications among a small group of local and international collaborators, while co-authorship networks illustrated tightly linked research teams working across institutions and borders.

Thematic analysis highlighted dominant keywords such as *COVID-19*, *Kenya*, *healthcare*, *vaccination*, and *impact*, suggesting a strong research emphasis on pandemic control, health system resilience, and policy effectiveness. Visualizations such as word clouds and collaboration networks further reinforced the highly collaborative and multidisciplinary nature of Kenya’s COVID-19 research landscape. Overall, this analysis underscores Kenya's active scientific contribution to global pandemic knowledge and identifies leading authors, institutions, and thematic gaps that can guide future research and health system investment priorities.
